## Proof

Define the function $F:\mathbb{R}\to[0,1]$ by
$$F(x)=\lambda\!\big(A\cap(-\infty,x]\big).$$

Since $A$ is measurable and $(-\infty,x]$ is measurable, the intersection is measurable, so $F$ is well defined. We establish three facts.

**Claim 1: $F$ is non-decreasing.** If $x\le y$, then $A\cap(-\infty,x]\subseteq A\cap(-\infty,y]$, so by monotonicity of $\lambda$, $F(x)\le F(y)$.

**Claim 2: $F$ is continuous.** It suffices to show right-continuity and left-continuity at every point. For $y>x$,
$$F(y)-F(x)=\lambda\!\big(A\cap(x,y]\big)\le \lambda\!\big((x,y]\big)=y-x,$$
so $F(y)\to F(x)$ as $y\downarrow x$ (right-continuity). Likewise, for $y<x$,
$$F(x)-F(y)=\lambda\!\big(A\cap(y,x]\big)\le x-y,$$
so $F(y)\to F(x)$ as $y\uparrow x$ (left-continuity). Hence $F$ is continuous on $\mathbb{R}$.

**Claim 3: $\lim_{x\to-\infty}F(x)=0$ and $\lim_{x\to+\infty}F(x)=\lambda(A)=1$.** For the first limit, $A\cap(-\infty,x]\subseteq(-\infty,x]$, so $0\le F(x)\le\lambda((-\infty,x])$; but $\lambda((-\infty,x])=\infty$ is not helpful directly. Instead note $A=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\big(A\cap(-\infty,-n]\big)\cup\big(A\cap(-n,n]\big)^{c}$... more cleanly: $A=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}(A\cap[-n,n])$ up to a null-adjustment, and $A\cap(-\infty,x]\downarrow\varnothing$ as $x\to-\infty$ in the sense that $A\cap(-\infty,-n]\downarrow\varnothing$. Since $\lambda(A\cap(-\infty,0])\le\lambda(A)=1<\infty$, continuity from above gives $\lambda(A\cap(-\infty,-n])\to\lambda(\varnothing)=0$. Thus $F(x)\to0$ as $x\to-\infty$.

For the second limit, $A\cap(-\infty,x]\uparrow A$ as $x\to+\infty$ (since $\bigcup_{n}(A\cap(-\infty,n])=A$). By continuity from below, $F(n)=\lambda(A\cap(-\infty,n])\to\lambda(A)=1$. Hence $F(x)\to1$ as $x\to+\infty$.

**Applying the Intermediate Value Theorem.** Since $F$ is continuous, $\lim_{x\to-\infty}F(x)=0<\tfrac12<1=\lim_{x\to+\infty}F(x)$, there exist real numbers $a,b$ with $F(a)<\tfrac12<F(b)$. By the Intermediate Value Theorem applied to the continuous function $F$ on $[a,b]$, there exists $x_0\in[a,b]$ such that
$$F(x_0)=\lambda\!\big(A\cap(-\infty,x_0]\big)=\frac12.$$

Set $B=A\cap(-\infty,x_0]$. Then $B\subseteq A$, $B$ is Lebesgue measurable (intersection of two measurable sets), and $\lambda(B)=\tfrac12$. $\blacksquare$

